{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the dap mcp writer at head 0385a59e, for request 6db548f0 and review request 95a9c539. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED. The writer's order, its rows and its witness hold. One path gives a false statement on the journey this writer exists for, and it also hides a retained answer. The repair is small, and it changes source, so the next head needs a new gate.\n\nI found this by reading the code, and I did not run it.\n\n## The change I request\n\n**A lost session stops before the code reads the record.** In `#sendAct`, the `session-lost` stop comes before `custody.keep` and `custody.find`, and it always passes `earlier: false`. So:\n\n1. A person calls an act, and the reply is lost. The record is `unknown`.\n2. The connection stays bad for more than about 50 seconds, so the session's renewal fails too. A lost reply and a failed renewal share a cause, so I expect them together.\n3. The person or the model calls again with the same key. The answer reads \"Nothing was sent.\"\n\nThat sentence is false about the earlier send. `Finding` has `earlier` for exactly this case, and the keep's own stops use it. A model that reads \"Nothing was sent\" can reasonably send a new request under a new key, and that is the second entry J1 exists to prevent. The `next` line does say to use the same key, which limits this and does not repair it.\n\nThe same stop also refuses a call whose record already holds a retained answer. That answer sits in custody on this machine and needs no session, and a repeat returned it before this head. `settle` still reaches it, because `#settle` does not go through `#sendAct`.\n\nThe repair: read the record first. A terminal record that is not a deliberate resubmission takes the plain path to its retained answer. An unresolved record that is not `prepared` stops with `earlier: true`. Add a control for each, and add a row to the note's restart table.\n\n## Your six questions\n\n1. **A stopped keep leaves a `prepared` record: acceptable.** The cap is 256, custody's `count` refusal names keys, and a retry clears each one: past `notAfter`, the bytes go and come back refused. It takes hundreds of calls under new keys during a long `room-full` to reach the cap, and that refusal says what to do.\n2. **`expired` from the keep: the decision is right, and the sentence is too strong.** Those bytes can also answer `unavailable`, `archived` or `mismatch`, or lose the reply. At the boundary they can land as a first entry, because the keep reads the membership object's clock and the act reads the target's. None of these does harm: no row is left behind, and custody holds the answer. Say \"they cannot make a second entry\" and drop \"can only\".\n3. **An agent whose first read fails: small enough, with one word changed.** \"Can keep one row\" understates it. `#notPeople` fills only when a read states the kind, so each send keeps a row until then. Each row still ends on its answer, so write \"can keep rows until a read states its kind\".\n4. **The restart limit on a chat surface: yes, a person meets it more often than on a page.** A stdio server starts with each chat, so the memory is shorter. It matters less here, because custody stays on disk and the same machine can still `settle`. What the person loses is the other device's view of that one request. I accept this as J1's limit, and `docs/mcp.md` should keep saying it.\n5. **`apps/views` not wired: right.** The ruling names `dap mcp`, and an unwitnessed writer is worse than a stated gap. Section 9 states it, and plan 032's \"every person's act\" stays open for that service and the CLI.\n6. **The restart table: I found no row wrong, from the code.** The second row's `expired` follows `Originals.keep`, which checks the time before it looks for a held row. The witness runs only the first row's `settle` and repeat. The table needs the new row from the change above.\n\n## One limit to state\n\n`holder = membership ?? s.repository` covers a failed read, as the comment says. It also covers a target that the inventory does not hold. For such a scope, the row goes to the configured room's membership, and `#endAnswered` follows the receipt there under that room's session. If that read is refused, the row stays after an accepted answer, until the seven days pass. I expect this to be rare, because a person's acts are almost all inside the room. Add it to the open limits on the next head.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n**Architecture.** Sound apart from the change above. The port keeps a hosted agent out, `hosted.ts` does not mention it, and my run of `hosted.scope.test.ts` passes. The order of `custody.keep`, the copy's keep and `custody.send` needs no change to custody, and taking the row's bytes from the stored record is the right choice.\n\n**Security.** No authority issue. The writer uses J1's routes under the member's own session, and a row needs that key's signature. The requested change is about truthful words and a duplicate act, not about access.\n\n**Simplification.** Acceptable. `#sendAct` holds several ownership conditions, and each has a control. Reading the record first should remove one branch of reasoning.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- **Read.** The whole diff of `packages/mcp/src`, `docs/mcp.md`, the design note's section 9, the implementation note, and `Custody.keep`. I did not read the tests' bodies, `mcp-second-device.mjs` or `custody.send` in full.\n- **Evidence.** `SHA256SUMS` hashes to `873e731a…`, each listed file verifies, and no file is writable. The gate's head is `1e782610`, it exited 0, and its log shows 3466 plain and 940 native tests. The witness and the controls record `c81afb6f`. From there to the checkpoint, under `packages/mcp`, the diff only adds the hosted loop's test. The 32 control rows distinguish. I read the witness table's source only for its failures, which are empty. I read those results and did not rerun them.\n- **Tests I ran at this head.** `mcp-original.scope.test.ts` and `hosted.scope.test.ts` pass 30 of 30. `lost-reply.test.ts` and `errors.test.ts` pass 8 of 8. `security-entry-points.test.mjs` passes 10 with 0 failures. `check-kernel-boundary` reports 127 sources and 0 refusals. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings, `check-doc-links` 0 broken links, and `check-commit-messages` 0 flagged. The worktree stayed clean.\n- **Not done.** I used no independent reader. No deployed Worker, real room or second physical device has run this writer.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1bccad440077c78ef373cdb18747c2eae7a79b9f","binding":"assigned","head":"0385a59eb8e5b90f1c6621371c0c02cd8d479341","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:fbacbbe9b08f69b66e2483e18171c5d696752875\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8c3989ce64531ec4b8cd5cc268af14e41296ee07\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:fbacbbe9b08f69b66e2483e18171c5d696752875","review_path":"reviewguard@1","stale":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0b69be52e983e88e205b2cb78a89b09ce4f86e4d, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1bccad440077c78ef373cdb18747c2eae7a79b9f, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1be034b80706519f25ff25ce2fff1923a7bc3899, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:40a6e34a9923c0828ea2547155bde1173d2e0b74, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:43658d75b937b109ee5a44af2ae397064b7d9a5e, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4867b14c184c28eddbc9f04cab2439c7823c3953, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4b5da1e6388b56549ecf3519c41808d3e4d74edc, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:51604aa5cd88b645c1e1ec8fde0d08acd912db09, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:585161d06277a378fbbc52ea8d89c5075e3ff5df